{"step_index":24,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-08-24T22:35:56Z","content":"Created At: 2026-08-25T04:05:56+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-08-25T04:05:56+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/renu5/.gemini/antigravity/brain/3afa10cf-ab2f-45bb-8f37-5ccb114e7ae0/.system_generated/steps/20/content.md`\nTotal Lines: 656\nTotal Bytes: 44598\nShowing lines 496 to 656\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n496: import '@tensorflow/tfjs-backend-cpu'; import * as tf from '@tensorflow/tfjs-core'; import * as tflite from '@tensorflow/tfjs-tflite'; const contentDetectionCanvas = document.createElement('canvas'); contentDetectionCanvas.width = 224; contentDetectionCanvas.height = 224; const contentDetectionCtx = contentDetectionCanvas.getContext('2d', {willReadFrequently: true}); const tfliteModel = await tflite.loadTFLiteModel('./content_detection_mobilenet_v3.tflite', {} ); const source = //video, image, imageBitmap, etc... contentDetectionCtx.drawImage(source, source.width/2-112, source.height/2-112, 224, 224, 0, 0, 224, 224 ); // Take the center 224x224 crop of the source const input = tf.div(tf.expandDims(img), 255); const outputTensor = tfliteModel.predict(input); const values = outputTensor.dataSync(); if(values[1] > values[0]) //animation else: //real life // I tried building a 3-network classifier, but ran into some accracy issues\n497: ```\n498: \n499: \n500: ```\n501: import '@tensorflow/tfjs-backend-cpu'; import * as tf from '@tensorflow/tfjs-core'; import * as tflite from '@tensorflow/tfjs-tflite'; const contentDetectionCanvas = document.createElement('canvas'); contentDetectionCanvas.width = 224; contentDetectionCanvas.height = 224; const contentDetectionCtx = contentDetectionCanvas.getContext('2d', {willReadFrequently: true}); const tfliteModel = await tflite.loadTFLiteModel('./content_detection_mobilenet_v3.tflite', {} ); const source = //video, image, imageBitmap, etc... contentDetectionCtx.drawImage(source, source.width/2-112, source.height/2-112, 224, 224, 0, 0, 224, 224 ); // Take the center 224x224 crop of the source const input = tf.div(tf.expandDims(img), 255); const outputTensor = tfliteModel.predict(input); const values = outputTensor.dataSync(); if(values[1] > values[0]) //animation else: //real life // I tried building a 3-network classifier, but ran into some accracy issues\n502: ```\n503: \n504: ### Why WebSR\n505: [Super resolution](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Super-resolution_imaging) is a technique which uses Neural Networks to \"reconstruct\" a high-resolution image from a low resolution image. Practically speaking, it provides better visual results than traditional upscaling algorithms like Bicubic, which is what devices and browsers usually use by default, at the cost of significantly more computation.\n506: A general rule of thumb is that bigger super-resolution networks (which use more computation) generally provide better results.\n507: This is why commercial AI upscaling software like [Topaz Labs](https://www.topazlabs.com/) can produce excellent quality results, though it can take 10 seconds or more on a dedicated GPU to upscale a single image. If the goal is to run super resolution on a video at 30 frames per second on mid-tier integrated graphcs cards in the browser, you need networks that run thousands of times faster. Computation is a huge challenge and a big reason that super resolution hasn't been more popular in web video.\n508: WebSR is an attempt to take on this challenge, and make super resolution practical in the browser. By taking advantage of the fact that super-resolution has proved relatively better at improving the video quality of Anime, Cartoons and Screen-sharing content, as well the faster graphics computation enabled by [WebGPU](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGPU_API), WebSR should be just-about practical enough for production video and WebRTC applicaitons in cases where bandwidth or video quality is an issue.\n509: See [this video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R6Ly0GYlXa4) for more info on super resolution on the web.\n510: \n511: ### How it works\n512: WebSR uses hand-written WebGPU shaders to implement Convolutional Neural Networks (like [this one](https://github.com/sb2702/websr/blob/main/src/networks/anime4k/cnn-2x-s.ts) from [Anime4K](https://github.com/bloc97/Anime4K)).\n513: When you create a WebSRinstance and call websr.start(), that starts a render-loop which will take in video frames, one by one, and upscale them using the Neural Network, before painting the result to a <canvas>.\n514: \n515: ```\n516: WebSR\n517: ```\n518: \n519: \n520: ```\n521: websr.start()\n522: ```\n523: \n524: \n525: ```\n526: <canvas>\n527: ```\n528: \n529: While there are many super-resolution networks and algorithms available, for a proof of concept, WebSR implements networks from [Anime4K](https://github.com/bloc97/Anime4K) as\n530: 1. It was designed for real time video\n531: 2. Already has GPU shader written in the similar glsl language\n532: 3. Already has wide community adoption.\n533: \n534: ```\n535: glsl\n536: ```\n537: \n538: This repo provides the [default production weights](https://github.com/sb2702/websr/blob/main/weights/anime4k/cnn-2x-s.json) asociated with the cnn-2x-s network directly from Anime4K.\n539: \n540: ```\n541: cnn-2x-s\n542: ```\n543: \n544: The plan is to add additional neural networks, whether from other open-source projects or building custom networks specifically for WebSR.\n545: \n546: ### Custom Training\n547: You can train your own custom super-resolution models tailored to your specific content type or quality requirements. The custom-training/ folder includes:\n548: \n549: ```\n550: custom-training/\n551: ```\n552: \n553: - Complete training pipeline - Jupyter notebooks for training TensorFlow models\n554: - Weight export tools - Convert trained models to WebGPU format\n555: - Training utilities - Data augmentation, degradation simulation, and visualization\n556: - Pre-configured architectures - Small, Medium, and Large network options\n557: Quick Start:\n558: 1. Prepare a dataset of high-quality images (500-1000+ images recommended)\n559: 2. Train your model using custom-training/Train_Model.ipynb\n560: 3. Export weights using custom-training/Export_Weights.ipynb\n561: 4. Use your custom weights in WebSR (see Quick Start example above)\n562: \n563: ```\n564: custom-training/Train_Model.ipynb\n565: ```\n566: \n567: \n568: ```\n569: custom-training/Export_Weights.ipynb\n570: ```\n571: \n572: For detailed instructions, see the [Custom Training README](https://github.com/sb2702/websr/blob/main/custom_training/README.md).\n573: Use Cases for Custom Training:\n574: - Content-specific optimization (e.g., medical imaging, satellite imagery)\n575: - Fine-tuning for specific art styles or game graphics\n576: - Balancing quality vs. performance for your target hardware\n577: - Training on proprietary content that differs from the provided weights\n578: \n579: ### Acknowledgements\n580: 1. [Anime4K](https://github.com/bloc97/Anime4K) for an excellent project\n581: 2. [WebGPU](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGPU_API) for enabling compute-intensive projects like this\n582: 3. [Blender foundation](https://studio.blender.org/films/hero/) for use of their hero movie in WebSR demos\n583: [Anime4K](https://github.com/bloc97/Anime4K)\n584: [WebGPU](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebGPU_API)\n585: [Blender foundation](https://studio.blender.org/films/hero/)\n586: \n587: ### Roadmap\n588: -  Add more upscaling networks (especially for other types of content - like real life, or screen-content)\n589: -  Dynamically switch between networks based on type of content\n590: -  Provide lower level controls (e.g. control over the render loop)\n591: -  OffscreenCanvas / worker thread support\n592: -  Custom training pipeline and documentation\n593: -  Write Mobile SDKs with similar functionality\n594: \n595: Javascript library for running real-time AI Upscaling in the browser\n596: [websr.dev](https://websr.dev)\n597: \n598: ### Topics\n599: [ai](https://github.com/topics/ai)\n600: [javascript-library](https://github.com/topics/javascript-library)\n601: [super-resolution](https://github.com/topics/super-resolution)\n602: [video](https://github.com/topics/video)\n603: \n604: ### Resources\n605: [Readme](https://github.com/sb2702/websr#readme-ov-file)\n606: [MIT license](https://github.com/sb2702/websr#MIT-1-ov-file)\n607: [Activity](https://github.com/sb2702/websr/activity)\n608: \n609: ### Forks\n610: [23 forks](https://github.com/sb2702/websr/forks)\n611: [Report repository](https://github.com/contact/report-content?content_url=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fsb2702%2Fwebsr&report=sb2702+%28user%29)\n612: \n613: - \n614:             [Terms](https://docs.github.com/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-of-service)\n615: \n616: - \n617:             [Privacy](https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement)\n618: \n619: - \n620:               [Security](https://github.com/security)\n621: \n622: - \n623:               [Status](https://www.githubstatus.com/)\n624: \n625: - \n626:             [Community](https://github.community/)\n627: \n628: - \n629:             [Docs](https://docs.github.com/)\n630: \n631: - \n632:             [Contact](https://support.github.com?tags=dotcom-footer)\n633: \n634: - \n635: \n636: \n637:       Manage cookies\n638: \n639: \n640: \n641: - \n642: \n643: \n644:         Do not share my personal information\n645: \n646: \n647: \n648: [Terms](https://docs.github.com/site-policy/github-terms/github-terms-of-service)\n649: [Privacy](https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-privacy-statement)\n650: [Security](ht